Claim:  $M$  is the center of the circumcircle of cyclic quadrilateral  $EDCF,$  implying  $MC=MD.$ Proof: For starters, note that  $AD = \tfrac{AB}{2}$  by construction, and  $AB = \tfrac{AC}{2}.$  To see why the latter is true, let  $O$  be the center of circle  $C_1.$  The angle formed when  $O$  is drawn to  $B$  is a right angle (Generally, the radius drawn to the tangency point is always a right angle). Furthermore, the perpendicular from the center to a chord of the circle bisects the chord, so  $OB$  bisects  $AC,$  implying the latter. Consider if we used Power of a Point on circle  $C_2.$  We obtain\[AB^2=AE \cdot AF=\frac{AB}{2} \cdot 2AB  = AD \cdot AC.\]Knowing  $AE \cdot AF = AD \cdot AC$  implies that triangles  $AED$  and  $ACF$  are similar. This further implies that  $\angle AED = \angle ACF,$  so quadrilateral  $EDCF$  is cyclic. Call its circumscribed circle  $C_3.$ Recall the perpendicular bisector of a chord on a circle will always go through the center. From this, it is easy to see that the mutual intersection of the perpendicular bisectors of two chords will be the center of the circle. In this case, we see that  $M$  is the center of  $C_3$  because of perpendicular bisectors of  $CF$  and  $DE.$  Furthermore, since  $M$  lies on  $CD,$   $CD$  must be the diameter of  $C_3,$  and we thus know  $MC=MD. \qquad \square$ Claim: The ratio  $AM/MC = 5/3.$ Proof: Let Let  $n$  represent the length of  $AD=DB.$  Recall that  $AB=BC,$  so we know that  $AC=4n.$  Furthermore, we obtain  $MC=MD=\tfrac{3}{2}n,$  as  $CD=3n.$  Therefore,\[\frac{AM}{MC} = \frac{n + \frac{3}{2}n}{\frac{3}{2}n} = \frac{\frac{5}{2}n}{\frac{3}{2}n} = \frac{5}{3},\]as desired.  $\qquad \square$ **Remark.** From my Oly Geo class a while ago..

