Using directed angles modulo  $180^{\circ}$ , we have  $$ \measuredangle X_1T'B=\measuredangle T'X_1X_2-\angle TBC=\measuredangle T'MY_2-\measuredangle T'MC=\measuredangle CMY_2 $$  and  $$ \measuredangle X_1T'D=\measuredangle Y_1ME=\measuredangle EMY_2=\measuredangle ET'Y_2 $$  It thus suffices to show that  $T'I$  bisects  $\angle DT'E$ . We have a spiral similarity at  $T$  sending  $X_1DX_2$  to  $Y_1EY_2$ . Hence,  $$ \frac{ET}{DT}=\frac{EY_2}{DX_2}=\frac{EI}{DI} $$  by the incenter/excenter lemma. We're done by the angle bisector theorem.

