Proposed by **psi241** (Nithid Anchaleenukoon). The following clean solution was given to me by Claire Zhang.

<details><summary>Solution</summary>The answer is  $\boxed{n!}$ .

We label the cells by coordinate  $(x,y)$ , where  $1\leq x,y\leq n$  and the bottom-left corner is  $(1,1)$ . Let  $S_1$ ,  $S_2$ ,  $S_3$ ,  $\dots$ ,  $S_n$  denote the piece with  $1,3,5,\dots,2n-1$  cells. 

Call the *main diagonal* the diagonal from the top left and bottom right. We first note that since each piece has odd size, it must contain at least one cell on the main diagonal. Since there are  $n$  pieces and  $n$  cells on the diagonal, each piece must cover exactly one cell on the diagonal.

We thus define a *signature* of a partition as the permutation  $\sigma$  of  $\{1,2,\dots,n\}$  such that  $S_{\sigma(i)}$  occupies the cell  $(i, n+1-i)$  in the main diagonal. The central claim in this problem is the following, which immediately implies the problem:

<span style="color:red">**Claim.**</span> The signature of a partition uniquely determines the entire partition.

*Proof.* Rather than drowning the reader with lots of notations, we demonstrate the solution by an example of  $n=6$  and  $\sigma = (3,1,6,2,5,4)$ . In this case, the argument works step-by-step by the following diagram.
[asy]
size(10cm,10cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
picture[] pics = {};
int n=6;
for(int i=0; i<n; ++i){
    picture pic;
    pics.push(pic);
}

int[] p = {3,1,6,2,5,4};
pen[] cols = {lightmagenta, lightred, green, paleblue, yellow, 0.5*orange+0.5*white};

for(int i=0; i<n; ++i){
	for(int j=i; j<n; ++j){
		for(int k=0; k<n-i; ++k){
			label(pics[j], (string) p[k], (k+0.5, n-0.5-k-i));
			fill(pics[j], box((k, n-1-k-i), (k+1,n-k-i)), cols[p[k]-1]);
			fill(pics[j], reflect((0,n),(n,0))*box((k, n-1-k-i), (k+1,n-k-i)), cols[p[k]-1]);
		}
	}
	for(int j=0; j<p.length; ++j){
		if(p[j] == i+1)
			p.delete(j);	
	}
}

for(int i=0; i<n; ++i){
	for(int j=0; j<=n; ++j){
        draw(pics[i], (0,j) -- (n,j), gray);
        draw(pics[i], (j,0) -- (j,n), gray);
    }
    for(int j=0; j<n; ++j){
        draw(pics[i], (j,n-j) -- (j+1,n-j), linewidth(1));
        draw(pics[i], (n-j,j) -- (n-j,j+1), linewidth(1));
    }
    draw(pics[i], (0,n)--(0,0)--(n,0), linewidth(1));
}


for(int i=0; i<3; ++i){
    add(pics[i].fit(3cm,3cm), (6*i,6));
}
for(int i=3; i<6; ++i){
    add(pics[i].fit(3cm,3cm), (6*(i-3),0));
}
[/asy]
The cells on the main diagonal  $x+y=n+1$  are already determined by  $\sigma$ . Next, consider the next diagonal  $x+y=n$ . Each piece, except  $S_1$ , must have at least one cell in this diagonal. Since this diagonal has  $n-1$  cells, each piece  $S_2,\dots,S_n$  must use exactly one cell. Due to connectivity, there is only one way to assign cells in this diagonal, as shown in the second diagram.

The same argument works repeatedly one diagonal at a time, from diagonals  $x+y=n-1$ ,  $\dots$ , to  $x+y=2$ , deleting  $S_2$ ,  $S_3$ ,  $\dots$ , to  $S_{n-1}$ . This force the entire partition, which clearly works. Hence, there is exactly one partition with signature  $\sigma$ .</details>

