\begin{align*}
    \binom{n}{0}-\binom{n}{p}+\binom{n}{2p}-\binom{n}{3p}+\dots &= a_0+a_p+a_{2p}+\dots  &= \frac{P(\omega^0)+P(\omega^1)+\dots+P(\omega^{p-1})}{p}  &= \frac{(1-\omega^0)^n+(1-\omega^1)^n+\dots+(1-\omega^{p-1})^n}{p}
\end{align*}
Notice that  $1-\omega^k$  be roots of   $$ Q(x) = (1-x)^{p-1}+(1-x)^{p-2}+\dots+(1-x)+1 $$   for each  $k \in [1,p-1]$  Moreover, leading coefficient of  $x^k$  in  $Q(x)$  is   $$ (-1)^k\bigg[\binom{p-1}{k}+\binom{p-2}{k}+\dots+\binom{k}{k}\Bigg] \stackrel{\text{Hockey-stick}}{=} (-1)^k\binom{p}{k+1} $$   or  $Q(x)=\sum\limits_{k=0}^{p-1}(-1)^k\binom{p}{k+1}x^k$   Let  $\alpha_1,\alpha_2,\dots,\alpha_{p-1}$  be pairwise distinct roots of  $Q(x)$   By Vieta's theorem we have   $$ x_i=\sum_{sym}\alpha_1\alpha_2\dots\alpha_i = \binom{p}{i} $$  it is suffices to show that  $p^{r+1} \mid \alpha_1^{n}+\alpha_2^n+\dots+\alpha_{p-1}^n$  for every  $n >r(p-1)$   First, notice that  $p \mid x_i$  for each  $i \in [1,p-1]$   and each newton-sum could be written in form of \[ \sum m x_1^{y_1}x_2^{y_2}\dots x_{p-1}^{y_{p-1}}\] where  $m \in \mathbb{Z}$  and  $y_i$  is non-negative integer for each  $i \in [1,p-1]$  \newpage By considering degree we have that  $y_1+2y_2+\dots+(p-1)y_{p-1}=n$   Then,   $$ y_1+y_2+\dots+y_{p-1} \geq \frac{n}{p-1} > r \implies y_1+y_2+\dots+y_{p-1} \geq r+1 $$   Finally we got,  $p^{r+1} \mid \alpha_1^{n}+\alpha_2^n+\dots+\alpha_{p-1}^n$  for every  $n > r(p-1)$  (since  $p \mid x_i$ )

