We claim that if  $S=\{qrp_1,qrp_2,\dots,qrp_{2019},p_1p_2\dots p_{2019}\}$  such that  $q>r>p_1>p_2>\dots>p_{2019}$  be prime number then  $S$  satisfy all three conditions.

The first condition is obvious.

Let consider the second condition. Let  $a\neq b$  be any elements of  $S$ . We have  $$ \gcd(a,b)=\begin{cases}
\gcd(qrp_i,qrp_j)=qr & \text{which is not prime.} 
\gcd(qrp_i,p_1p_2\dots p_{2019})=p_i & \text{which is prime.}
\end{cases} $$  All primes in  $\{\gcd(a, b) : a, b \in S, a \neq b\}$  are  $p_1,p_2,\dots ,p_{2019}$ . Thus  $S$  also satisfy the second condition.

Let consider the third condition. Note that  $qr\mid ab$  for every  $a,b\in S,a<b$  since at least one of  $a,b$  must be  $qrp_i$ . 
And so  $qr\mid \sum\limits_{a,b\in A; a<b} ab$  which make  $\sum\limits_{a,b\in A; a<b}ab$  to not be a prime power.  $S$  satisfy the third condition.

