A miraculously simple solution can be done by using mathematical induction on  $n\in\mathbb{N}$ .

First, if  $f\equiv c$  is a constant polynomial. We may take  $k=a^c\in\mathbb{N}$  so that  $n\mid a^{f(a^c)}-a^c$ , and if  $a=1$ , then  $k=1$  works for all  $n\in\mathbb{N}$ .
Henceforth, we may assume that  $f$  is not a constant polynomial and  $a>1$ .
[rule]
We will prove a stronger statement: "For each  $n\in\mathbb{N}$ , there exists infinitely many  $k\in\mathbb{N}$  such that  $n\mid a^{f(k)}-k$ ."
Consider  $n=1$ , it is easy to see that any  $k\in\mathbb{N}$  works.
Consider  $n=2$ , there are infinitely many  $t\in\{a,3a,5a,\dots\}$  such that  $f(t)\neq 0$ . With the choice  $k=t$ , we have  $$ a^{f(k)}-k\equiv a^{f(t)}-a\equiv 0\pmod{2}. $$  Let  $\ell\in\mathbb{N}_{>1}$ . Assume that for each  $m\leqslant \ell$ , there exists infinitely many  $M\in\mathbb{N}$  such that  $m\mid a^{f(M)}-M$ .
Write  $\ell+1=p_1^{a_1}p_2^{a_2}\cdots p_s^{a_s} q_1^{b_1}q_2^{b_2}\cdots q_t^{b_t}$  where  $s,t\in\mathbb{N}$  such that  $p_1,p_2,\dots,p_s,q_1,q_2,\dots,q_t$  are distinct prime numbers,  $a_1,a_2,\dots,a_s,b_1,b_2,\dots,b_t$  are positive integers and  $p_i\mid a$  for each  $1\leqslant i\leqslant s$ , but  $q_i\nmid a$  for each  $1\leqslant i\leqslant t$ .
For brevity, denote some variables:  $$ P:= p_1^{a_1}p_2^{a_2}\cdots p_s^{a_s},\quad Q:= q_1^{b_t}q_2^{b_2}\cdots q_t^{b_t}. $$  Note that  $\phi(Q)<Q\leqslant\ell+1$ , we can choose large enough integer  $g>P$  such that   $$ \phi(Q)\mid a^{f(g)}-g $$  Consider the choice  $k=PQ\phi(Q)\lambda+a^{f(g)}$  where  $\lambda\in\mathbb{N}$ . We will show that  $P\mid a^{f(g)}$ .
Notice that  $$ \nu_{p_i}(a^{f(g)})=f(g)\nu_{p_i}(a)\geqslant g\nu_{p_i}(a)>P\cdot 1>\nu_{p_i}(P) $$  for each integer  $1\leqslant i\leqslant s$ . Which means that  $P\mid a^{f(g)}$ . In consequence,  $$ P\mid a^{f(PQ\phi(Q)\lambda+a^{f(g)})}-PQ\phi(Q)\lambda -a^{f(g)}, $$  since  $PQ\phi(Q)\lambda+a^{f(g)}\geqslant g$ . It remains to show  $Q\mid a^{f(k)}-k$ .
First, with  $\phi(Q)\mid a^{f(g)}-g$  and some polynomial properties, it can be derived that
\begin{align*}
    f(PQ\phi(Q)\lambda+a^{f(g)})&\equiv f(a^{f(g)})\equiv f(g)\pmod{\phi(Q)}
    PQ\phi(Q)\lambda+a^{f(g)}&\equiv a^{f(g)}\pmod{Q}
\end{align*}
By Euler- $\phi$  theorem with  $\gcd(a,Q)=1$ , we have
\begin{align*}
a^{f(k)}-k&\equiv a^{f(PQ\phi(Q)\lambda+a^{f(g)})}-PQ\phi(Q)\lambda-a^{f(g)}\pmod{Q}
&\equiv a^{f(a^{f(g)})}-a^{f(g)}\pmod{Q}
&\equiv a^{f(g)}-a^{f(g)}\equiv 0\pmod{Q}
\end{align*}
Since  $\gcd(P,Q)=1$ , so  $(\ell+1)=PQ\mid a^{f(PQ\phi(Q)\lambda+a^{f(g)})}-PQ\phi(Q)\lambda+a^{f(g)}$ .
We may choose infinitely many  $\lambda\in\mathbb{N}$ . Hence, our inductive step is done.

In conclusion, for each  $n\in\mathbb{N}$ , there is some  $k\in\mathbb{N}$  such that  $n\mid a^{f(k)}-k$  as desired.

<details><summary>Omitted fact.</summary>Here, since  $f\in\mathbb{N}_0[x]$ , it's well-known that  $f(m)\equiv f(m+kn)\pmod{n}$  for all  $k\in\mathbb{N}$ .</details>
<details><summary>Comment.</summary>The idea behind this was to notice that we may shift  $k\mapsto k+jn$ . As if  $\gcd(a,n)=1$ , then the cycle of  $a^{f(k+jn)}$  is with the size  $\phi(n)$ . But, it was also required to perceive a fact that  $k\equiv a^{\ell}\pmod{n}$  for some  $\ell\in\mathbb{N}_0$ . Which, on the latter, turns this problem into the congruence equation  $\ell\equiv f(a^{\ell}+jn)\pmod{\phi(n)}$ . So, the idea of mapping  $\ell$  to some  $f(g)$  comes in!, and it is easy to deal with an induction. But, the one major issue is that  $\gcd(a,n)$  is not always be  $1$ . And, if I didn't do anything wrong, then the above solution should preserve the stated motivation.</details>

