First notice that since the circle (let us say that it has centre  $O$ ) is tangent to  $BC$  at its midpoint,  $OM \perp BC$  and  $OM$  is a radius of the circle. This also means that  $O$  is on the altitude  $AM$ . Now, because  $DE$  meets  $OM$  at  $P$ , and  $OM$  is a radius and  $DE$  is a chord of the circle,  $DE \perp OM$ . Then since  $OM \perp DE$  and  $OM \perp BC$ , we have  $DE$  parallel to  $BC$ , and so  $\bigtriangleup ADE \sim \bigtriangleup ABC$ , or  $ \bigtriangleup ADE$  is equilateral. 

Extend the line  $AM$  past  $A$  to hit the circle at a point  $N$ . We have  $NM = 2\times OM = 4$ . Let  $PE = PD = x$ . Then  $AP = \sqrt{3}\times PE = \sqrt{3}x$ , and  $PM = AM - AP = 3 -  \sqrt{3}x$ . Also,  $NP = NM - PM = 4 -(3 -  \sqrt{3}x) = 1+\sqrt{3}x$ . 

From power of a point,  $PD \times PE = NP \times PM \Rightarrow x^2 = (1+\sqrt{3}x)(3 -  \sqrt{3}x)$  and so  $x = \frac{1}{4}(\sqrt{3}+\sqrt{15})$  since  $x$  is positive. 

Thus  $DE = 2 \times PE = 2 \times x = 2\times  \frac{1}{4}(\sqrt{3}+\sqrt{15}) = \frac{1}{2}(\sqrt{3}+\sqrt{15})$ .

We have:  $AM = \sqrt{3}BM \Rightarrow 3= \sqrt{3}BM \Rightarrow BM = \sqrt3$  and so  $BC = 2\times BM = 2\sqrt{3}$ .

\[A_{\bigtriangleup ABC} = \frac{\sqrt{3}}{4} (s^2) = \frac{\sqrt{3}}{4} (BC^2) = 3\sqrt{3}\]
\[A_{\bigtriangleup ADE} = \frac{\sqrt{3}}{4} (s^2) = \frac{\sqrt{3}}{4} (DE^2) = \frac{\sqrt{3}}{8} (9+3\sqrt{5})\]

and so

\[ \frac{A_{\bigtriangleup ABC}}{A_{\bigtriangleup ADE}} = \frac{ 3\sqrt{3}}{ \frac{\sqrt{3}}{8} (9+3\sqrt{5})} = 6 - 2\sqrt{5}\]</details>

